WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.659
Alright, so we've talked about how you
can clone a repository so you can work

00:00:03.659 --> 00:00:06.899
on it in your own development
environment. Just in case you're

00:00:06.899 --> 00:00:10.410
interested in working in the with the
command line a little bit, here are some

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.429
of the more common commands that you
would use with Git. Now, these are just my

00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:18.330
opinion and you will need a lot more
commands than just this, but this will

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:23.670
get you off to a good start. 'git clone' is
what we talked about earlier this is how

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:28.680
you can clone a repository down to your
local machine. You'll notice all of these

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:33.420
start off with 'git' ("G-I-T") that's an
important piece to remember. Now you

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.809
don't have to memorize all of these at
once but I do want to kind of explain

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:41.730
them just a little bit. 'git pull', remember
how we talked about the remote

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:46.320
repository that everyone has their
changes, y'know, uploaded to that? When you run

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.770
'git pull' your bringing down the
changes that some of your colleagues

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:54.930
might have made and pushed up to the
remote repository. 'git branch' is how you

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.120
create a branch on your local machine.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.699
Remember we don't want to make commits on
master want to create a branch first. We

00:01:02.699 --> 00:01:07.200
check out to that branch with 'git checkout'. When we do that we're letting Git

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.130
know that any commits that are made we
want them to be on that specific branch.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:16.710
'git status' is maybe my favorite command
you can run 'git status' at any time and

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.900
it will tell you exactly what's going on.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:23.759
You can use this at any point in time. So
following the GitHub workflow a little

00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:28.590
bit we created a branch and we made some
changes and we want to store them as

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:35.220
commits we use 'git add' before we use 'git
commit'. Now,  we'll get into the complexity

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.780
of this a little bit on the next video
but it's important to know that it is a

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:45.240
two-step commit. Once you've
made the changes locally you can push

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:49.829
them up to the remote repository just
like you pulled them down with 'git push'.

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:54.930
So again, you don't need to memorize all
of these right now but just a little

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.630
introduction so that maybe the command
line isn't so scary.

